content.text:
That answer has a hole in it I want to close before I record it: **an entity with no identifier and no functionality is indistinguishable from a comment.** So "carved out" needs to name an artifact. Also — when you say "interface", I can't tell if you mean a Java `interface` (a named contract with no implementation, which Spring wires an implementation into at runtime) or "a boundary/seam" in the general sense.

Three concrete renderings, cheapest first:

---

❓ **Q1a** — **What form does the carve-out actually take?**

**(a) Documentation only.** `CONTEXT.md` gains a `User` entry marked as deliberately-unmodelled, and the `Account` definition says ownership is a future concern. Zero code. Cost: ~10 minutes.

**(b) An empty marker in code.** A Java `interface AccountOwner {}` with no methods, or an unused nullable `owner_id` column. Cost: ~20 minutes.

**(c) A live seam at the call site.** One named contract that *is actually invoked* on every transfer, but whose only implementation currently does nothing:

```java
public interface OwnershipPolicy {
    void assertMayTransferFrom(Account source);
}

@Component  // Spring finds this and injects it wherever OwnershipPolicy is asked for
class PermitAllOwnershipPolicy implements OwnershipPolicy {
    public void assertMayTransferFrom(Account source) { /* no owners modelled yet */ }
}
```

The transfer service calls `ownershipPolicy.assertMayTransferFrom(source)` before debiting. No identifier, no data, no `User` table — but the *exact line* where ownership will one day be enforced exists, is named, and is covered by tests. Replacing it later means writing one new class; no existing call site moves. Cost: ~30 minutes.

➡️ **(c).** It's the only one of the three that isn't dead weight — (a) is invisible to a code reviewer reading the source, and (b) is speculative generality: a marker nothing references reads as over-engineering, not forethought. (c) is the version where "its place is clearly carved out" is literally true, and it's cheap.

One flag: **(c) partly pre-empts Q2.** An ownership seam is the natural place auth plugs into, so choosing it makes "auth is out of scope" a slightly weaker claim — I'll re-frame Q2 accordingly once you answer.

